{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED for exact head aa74d9853eecc6a4725768a4861dd42220543080.\n\nThe main direction is sound: inventory should consume an independently released nested jsonataddl module; chess is extend-first because v0.1.3 has no declared capability or host-context seam; and the workroom remains native. I independently confirmed Tailapps tag commit e82a5b06775823062cda76859a57d22945dc09c6, current local main/origin/main a79901f377c2fde3fe583c31a1924651cf00b2d4, no jsonataddl/go.mod/go.sum change from v0.1.3 to that main, the fixed JSONata allowlist, closed identity components, single-writer check, and the current spike's opaque-event behavior. make docs, git diff --check, clean-tree, exact-base, and branch-equals-origin gates pass.\n\nMaterial corrections:\n1. The inventory rewrite as specified cannot compile. The current receive_stock and reserve_stock folds both write stock, while v0.1.3 rejects multiple writers. Specify one analytic inventory fold over inventory_event, writing stock and reservations and branching on kind.\n2. Specify result mapping. The current spike skips an opaque schema, advances the frontier, and emits no gitseq_decisions row. A normalizer skip must emit no private event and must not become an application-level ineffective decision or fact; the single analytic fold result for a recognized source record supplies the one Gitseq decision and its facts. Reconcile section 2.7, section 2.10, and Corpus C so per-record equality is actually achievable.\n3. Specify the full EvaluationInput.Event passed to the normalizer, including decoded payload and ordered rests_on. HostEvent.Fields only controls scalar read parameters; it neither validates nor mechanically identity-binds these non-scalar inputs. Bind their conversion explicitly in host.canonicalization and expand I5 or the appropriate upstream gap.\n4. Split the cross-repository I8. Inventory adoption and binding land in gitseq-inventory under that repository's workroom and target ref. A later Gitseq-local delivery removes spike/jsonataddl and the direct dependency and updates docs/reference/architecture.md in that same exact head. Do not put a contract-changing deletion in one head and its architecture update in a later I9.\n5. Remove the obsolete offline-main precondition and limit: current Tailapps main is locally verified and does not change this package or its pins. Correct “six” statements to seven.\n6. Narrow the mechanical-identity claim. The dialect configuration is hashed, but core and host implementation semantics still rely on deliberately updated component values, and Meta is presently undeclared. Every dependency pin move must remain corpus-gated even if component strings do not move. Also do not attribute a whole-Gitseq dependency ban to the fold study; it prohibited Tailapps/JSONata in the workroom fold.\n\nArchitecture: layers 4, 5, and 6 are correctly identified, and the proposed core/host separation is promising, but the present host contract omits non-scalar input semantics and the follow-on plan violates the same-head architecture rule. Contract not yet approvable.\n\nSecurity: the default-deny SQL authorizer, no ambient application plugins, fixed function allowlist, rollback boundary, and chess no-shortcut conclusion preserve the intended trust boundaries. The undeclared payload/rests_on/Meta conversion is an unresolved identity and input-validation boundary, so security approval must wait for the explicit contract.\n\nSimplification: use one inventory fold, one decision mapping, and repository-local deliveries. Those changes remove an impossible two-writer topology and an unexecutable cross-repository task without weakening any condition. The long reconciliation tables are justified evidence and need not be shortened mechanically.\n\nThis is a design-only review. It authorizes neither adoption nor implementation.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:de15fcee8bf00c264126be